WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:01:05.650
Planning connects your work to other people’s plans. Planning means deciding what result is needed and working out the actions, people, information, resources and timing required to achieve it. A useful plan also identifies how progress will be checked and what must happen when a condition changes. Your plan affects other people. A stakeholder is a person or function whose work, decision or interests affect or are affected by the plan. Before promising a time or result, you check their existing commitments, the inputs they must supply and whether they have accepted the work. The parts must fit together like a puzzle; an empty space in your diary is not evidence that everyone else is ready. For example, a decision meeting depends on more than an invitation. The executive needs preparation time, finance and operations need time to supply current papers, and Daniel needs a review point to identify gaps. If one input is late, the people responsible must understand the effect and agree the next action. Planning makes those dependencies visible rather than leaving others to discover a conflict.
